So for the, you know, I know some of you

00:12.000 --> 00:14.000
when we're talking about TypeScript,

00:14.000 --> 00:16.000
a lot of you probably heard a lot of, well,

00:16.000 --> 00:20.960
talks about the many reasons why TypeScript should be used.

00:20.960 --> 00:22.800
However, for me personally, I've always

00:22.800 --> 00:25.760
wanted TypeScript to be well, a little bit.

00:25.760 --> 00:27.360
I always thought a little bit scary to get into,

00:27.360 --> 00:28.320
because when people talked about it,

00:28.320 --> 00:29.600
they were talking about all in migration

00:29.600 --> 00:30.560
to that kind of stuff.

00:30.560 --> 00:31.680
So when I designed this workshop,

00:31.680 --> 00:33.360
I really designed for people who really

00:33.360 --> 00:36.080
have been curious to use TypeScript with view for a long time.

00:36.080 --> 00:38.160
And especially with view too, that wasn't as easily,

00:38.160 --> 00:39.600
like it was kind of hard to do that,

00:39.600 --> 00:41.680
because it felt like you were doing hacks a lot of the time.

00:41.680 --> 00:43.440
So then people were fighting with TypeScript,

00:43.440 --> 00:46.240
and ultimately wasn't a great experience.

00:46.240 --> 00:48.160
On the other hand, on the other spectrum, when it comes to who

00:48.160 --> 00:49.920
this workshop is for, we have people who've been

00:49.920 --> 00:52.000
itching to use TypeScript, right?

00:52.000 --> 00:54.880
And so they want to learn the fundamentals of how they work together.

00:54.880 --> 00:57.120
And everyone else in between this,

00:57.120 --> 00:58.800
this is who the workshop is designed for.

00:58.800 --> 01:02.400
To get you all really a flavor of what view with TypeScript is

01:02.400 --> 01:04.240
liked, but the key thing to understand, though,

01:04.240 --> 01:07.680
is that this is not a workshop that's designed to make you an expert

01:07.680 --> 01:08.320
in TypeScript.

01:08.320 --> 01:11.120
There are plenty of other wonderful resources on the Friend

01:11.120 --> 01:12.480
and Master's workshop platform.

01:12.480 --> 01:14.960
So if you want to get a really deep dive into TypeScript,

01:14.960 --> 01:16.080
be sure to check those out.

01:16.080 --> 01:18.000
But we'll really be focusing on integrations

01:18.000 --> 01:19.200
and getting people up to speed.

01:19.200 --> 01:20.880
So even if you don't know any TypeScript,

01:20.880 --> 01:21.680
that is totally fine.

01:21.680 --> 01:24.320
You'll be just at home here.

01:27.120 --> 01:29.520
OK, so once again, it's kind of reiterate.

01:29.520 --> 01:34.000
For TypeScript has taken the front end ecosystem by storm.

01:34.000 --> 01:37.680
But again, from my perspective, a lot of times when

01:37.680 --> 01:40.720
we have these popular tools, people do that whole like,

01:40.720 --> 01:42.560
when you're holding in hammer, everything looks like a nail.

01:42.560 --> 01:44.480
So everyone's to put TypeScript in everything.

01:44.480 --> 01:46.880
There are issues with that.

01:46.880 --> 01:49.280
And so I remember just listening to all these talks and thinking,

01:49.280 --> 01:51.120
well, some things kind of not right.

01:51.120 --> 01:53.120
Like, is it really as wonderful as it is,

01:53.120 --> 01:55.920
given that people would talk about all these theoretical benefits

01:55.920 --> 01:56.400
they would get.

01:56.400 --> 01:59.840
And then at the end of the day, they were fighting all these,

01:59.840 --> 02:04.480
whether it's type, type inferences, all that fun stuff.

02:04.480 --> 02:08.320
And so the whole premise of my workshop for me

02:08.320 --> 02:11.120
was what we could just use TypeScript without going all in on it.

02:11.120 --> 02:13.120
How can we progressively move towards it,

02:13.120 --> 02:16.560
use what works well without feeling like it's like the gospel

02:16.560 --> 02:19.920
for all code bases?

02:19.920 --> 02:23.120
And so the key thing to remember as we go through the rest of this,

02:23.120 --> 02:24.960
workshop is that your code is not inferior

02:25.040 --> 02:26.160
because you don't use TypeScript.

02:26.160 --> 02:28.560
I feel like sometimes a lot of times it is spoken in that way

02:28.560 --> 02:29.840
that if you're not using TypeScript somehow,

02:29.840 --> 02:31.200
you're like behind the curve.

02:31.200 --> 02:33.520
And at the end of the day, we have to remember that

02:33.520 --> 02:35.360
TypeScript, like a lot of other tools and texts,

02:35.360 --> 02:37.600
they come with trade-offs and the key thing is whether or not

02:37.600 --> 02:39.520
it's solving problems for you and your team.

02:39.520 --> 02:42.160
If you're a junior team, have perfect tests and everything's working,

02:42.160 --> 02:45.360
and there's no reason to move to TypeScript,

02:45.360 --> 02:47.120
you shouldn't feel pressure too.

02:47.120 --> 02:50.000
So I think that's just a key thing to remember as we talk about this.

